.TH BAS 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bas \- minimal BASIC interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bas
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bas
is a small, incomplete BASIC interpreter loosely modelled on the
Seventh Edition
.BR bas .
Numbered program lines are compiled into an internal instruction
buffer and executed with the
.B run
command.
Immediate (unnumbered) statements are not implemented.
.PP
If
.I file
is given, its lines are read and processed as though they had been
typed, and
.B bas
then continues reading from standard input.
Loading a file does not run it.
.PP
In interactive use,
.B bas
prints the prompt
.B >
before each input line.
.SS Commands
A line that does not start with a digit is a command.
Commands are lower case and must appear alone on the line.
.TP
.B list
Print the stored program in line-number order.
.TP
.B run
Execute the stored program from its lowest-numbered line.
When the program ends,
.B Done
is printed.
.TP
.BR exit ", " quit
Exit
.BR bas .
.PP
Any other unnumbered line prints
.B "Immediate mode not fully implemented"
and is otherwise ignored.
.SS Program lines
A line beginning with a decimal number, followed by a space and a
statement, is stored under that line number, replacing any existing
line with the same number.
A line number with no statement text is ignored; it does not delete
the line.
Keywords and function names are case-insensitive.
.PP
Only one statement per line is compiled; text after a
.B :
separator is discarded.
.PP
The following statements are recognised:
.TP
\fBPRINT\fP \fIitem\fP [\fB;\fP \fIitem\fP]...
Print string literals in double quotes and numeric expressions.
Items are separated by
.B ;
or
.BR , ,
which behave identically.
Numbers are printed with
.BR %g
formatting surrounded by single spaces.
A trailing
.B ;
or
.B ,
suppresses the final newline.
.TP
.BI "LET " var " = " expr
Assign to a variable.
.B LET
may be omitted.
.TP
.BI "GOTO " line
Jump to a line.
.TP
.BI "IF " expr " THEN " line
.TQ
.BI "IF " expr " GOTO " line
Jump to
.I line
if
.I expr
is non-zero.
.TP
.BI "FOR " var " = " expr " TO " expr " \fR[\fPSTEP " expr \fR]\fP
Start a loop; the default step is 1.
.TP
.BI "NEXT " var
End a loop.
.I var
must match the innermost active
.BR FOR .
.TP
.BI "GOSUB " line
Call a subroutine.
.TP
.B RETURN
Return from a subroutine.
.PP
Unrecognised statements are silently discarded.
.SS Expressions
Expressions use double-precision numbers, variables, parentheses and
the operators
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR * ,
and
.BR / .
There are no comparison or logical operators and no unary minus.
Division by zero reports an error and yields 0.
.PP
Variable names consist of letters and digits; only the first three
characters are significant.
Variables are initially 0.
There are no string variables and no arrays.
.PP
The built-in functions, each taking one argument in parentheses, are
.BR INT ,
.BR SQR ,
.BR SIN ,
.BR COS ,
.BR ABS ,
.BR RND ,
.BR LOG ,
.BR EXP ,
and
.BR ATN .
.B RND
ignores its argument and returns a pseudo-random value in [0,1].
.SS Diagnostics
Runtime errors are printed to standard output as
.BI "Error at " line ": " message \fR.\fP
Errors that stop the program include jumps to undefined lines,
.B NEXT
without
.BR FOR ,
and
.B RETURN
without
.BR GOSUB .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
End of standard input, or an
.B exit
or
.B quit
command.
.TP
.B 1
.I file
could not be opened, or an option was given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ bas
> 10 FOR I = 1 TO 3
> 20 PRINT "I = "; I
> 30 NEXT I
> run
I =  1
I =  2
I =  3

Done
> quit
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bc (1),
.BR dc (1)
.SH BUGS
.B bas
is incomplete: there are no relational operators (so
.B IF
can only test for non-zero), no unary minus, no
.BR INPUT ,
.BR END ,
.BR REM ,
or
.BR DIM ,
no immediate mode, and no way to delete a line.
.PP
Replaced lines are not freed from the instruction buffer, which holds
8192 instructions in total; the program table holds 600 lines and the
symbol table 200 variables.
The instruction and line tables are not bounds-checked.
.PP
A malformed expression such as
.B "PRINT )"
can make the compiler loop without consuming input.
